  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 308 AM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nWeak ridging aloft builds into the region as the shortwave departs \nlate Sunday, leading to a dry and seasonable start to the next work \nweek. Meanwhile, a much more potent trough spanning much of the \nwestern CONUS will track downstream and approach the Mississippi \nValley. The deep southerly flow that results from such an \namplified pattern will allow near-record moisture to transport \npoleward along a cold front to force widespread showers and a \nfew thunderstorms. While ensemble members and deterministic \nguidance agree on the broad strokes of how this system will \nevolve as it tracks east, a few key details are less certain. \nRegardless of these differences, the system is forecast to bring\na widespread, beneficial rain to the entire region early \nTuesday through Thursday. Exact values will vary by location, \nand hinge to some extent on the less-certain aspects of the \nwave's evolution, but NBM and LREF probabilities of 1.00 inches \nare now in the 50-80% range (highest across central and \nnortheast Missouri). While lower elsewhere, appreciable rainfall\nis still forecast.\n\nThe most notable difference evident in the ensembles, according to \nEOF patterns of the individual members, is the amplitude and \nstructure of this trough. How it evolves and interacts with a \nreinforcing shortwave amidst the polar jet stream will affect the \ntrack and strength of any surface features. Surface low tracks vary \nin the guidance from South Dakota to Kansas by Tuesday morning, \nimpacting the system's speed and available instability. As of now, \nmost guidance either 1) keeps a single amplified wave that \nsuppresses instability too far south or 2) shears off a second wave \nfrom the base of the main trough that causes additional surface \ncyclogenesis to pinch off instability. Either way, despite ample \ndeep-layer shear, severe thunderstorm chances largely evade the Mid-\nMississippi Valley. While model soundings vary a bit in how \nsaturated the column becomes, and for how long it stays saturated, \nthe factor that may inhibit heavy rain more is the lack of \ninstability that was already discussed. While there is still time \nfor shifts in the trough structure/surface low track to favor a \nparticular hazard, currently there is little to suggest this system \nbrings anything other than needed rain.\n\nA growing number of ensemble solutions suggest the wave closes as it \ntracks overhead, making it more sluggish and difficult to advance \ndownstream. As a result, rain chances will linger to some extent \nthrough much of the upcoming week. Exactly when rain chances drop \noff, and how much of a break we get before another system, is very \nuncertain with the upper-level patten depicted in wildly-different \nways beyond Wednesday. The persistent rain chances and cloud cover, \ncoupled with at least some more polar-type airmasses approaching, \nallow us to continue our trend cooler as we close the first week of \nNovember. Still, with normal temperatures also cooling, we likely \nwon't see temperatures too out of the ordinary for this time of year.",
